course: dictionary
lesson: 8741
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning. The dictionary gives servant three main meanings, and the words around it show you which one the writer means. Always check the clues before you choose.
explain: «The servant who buried his talent said he was afraid of his master.» The clue is the master: a servant works for and serves someone else. This meaning is about serving others, not necessarily cooking or cleaning.
explain: «There are servants everywhere: the Rabbit's housemaid Mary Ann, his gardener Pat, the Duchess's cook and the Frog-Footman who should open doors.» The clues are housemaid, gardener, cook and footman, all jobs done in a big house. So here servant means a person paid to work in someone else's home, especially in the past.
fact: A servant is a person who works for and serves others.
fact: In old stories and history lessons, servants in rich families cooked, cleaned and served food.
fact: A «civil servant» or «public servant» is someone who works for the government.
fact: In old letters, «Your humble servant» is a polite way to end, and it is not about a job at all.
fact: «Servants» is the plural of servant, so one servant becomes many servants.
exercise: «The servant who buried his talent said he was afraid of his master.» Who does the servant work for? | A government office | A tool for digging | *His master | The servant's own family
exercise: Mira says a «civil servant» is someone who cooks for a king. What does civil servant really mean? | A castle guard | Someone who cooks for a king | *Someone who works for the government | A lady's maid
exercise: Find and copy one job from this list: «the Rabbit's housemaid Mary Ann, his gardener Pat, the Duchess's cook». | castle | rabbit | duchess | *gardener
test: «The Woman says they need no more friends or servants.» Which meaning of servant fits here? | A government worker | *A person who works for and serves others | A kind of blade
test: Which is the plural of servant? | servant's | *servants | servant-men | servanted
exam: Which phrase means the rooms where servants live? | public servant | *servants' quarters | civil servant | Your humble servant
